text:
describe who is missing, not just how much;
fill a missing number with a mean or median, and a missing category with its mode, and say what each does to the distribution;
argue for keeping “Unknown” as a category;
impute a value from a patient’s other measurements;
measure how wrong an imputation was, and say why it could not have been right.

put variables measured in different units onto a comparable scale, three different ways;
say what an extreme value does to each scaler, and to the other patients;
use a log transformation to change the shape of a skewed variable;
derive a clinically meaningful feature from columns you already have.

combine imputation, encoding, scaling and a model into one pipeline;
look inside a fitted pipeline and say what each step learned, and from which patients;
say what happens when the fitted pipeline meets new patients;
give a reason for the order of the preprocessing steps.
